<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=astrolabe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astrolabe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">4th century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An astrolabe is an astronomical instrument that serves as a star chart and model of the visible sky, used to measure the altitude of stars and find latitude; it dates from classical antiquity, and Theon of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> (c. 335–405) wrote a detailed treatise on it. The earliest extant treatise is that of John Philoponus, of about 550, and the earliest surviving astrolabe is dated 927–928. Muslim astronomers added azimuth scales and used it to fix prayer times and the qibla, and it served European astronomers and navigators into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astrolabe.jpg" alt="Astrolabe" width="320" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khalili Collections · CC BY-SA 3.0 igo · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khalili_Collection_Islamic_Art_sci_0430_back.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>4th century CE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Alexandria</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Theon of Alexandria · <a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt</a></dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Whether Ptolemy or earlier astronomers used it is debated, and its attribution to Hypatia rests on a misreading.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">Babylonian astronomy<span>c. 1800 BCE</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/euclids-elements">Euclid's Elements<span>c. 300 BCE</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-beg-observatory">Ulugh Beg Observatory<span>1420s</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=astrolabe">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
